WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.629
HAASS: Welcome to the Council on Foreign Relations.

00:00:03.629 --> 00:00:09.269
Tonight is part of a series of what to me
are some of the most important things we do

00:00:09.269 --> 00:00:17.680
here, which is celebrate the publication of
a book done by, in this case, two of our fellows.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:21.680
And it's a chance to give them a-it's an opportunity
to give them a chance to talk about it and

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:26.039
give you a chance to raise questions about
it as well.

00:00:26.039 --> 00:00:33.450
Tonight's book has the provocative name of
"War by Other Means"-you will understand the

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:36.840
origin-and subtitled "Geoeconomics and Statecraft."

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:43.829
The two authors, shockingly enough, are sitting
next to me: Jennifer Harris, who is a senior

00:00:43.829 --> 00:00:51.070
fellow here at the Council on Foreign Relations,
and Bob Blackwill, who is also a senior fellow,

00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:56.059
sits in the Henry A. Kissinger position here
for U.S. Foreign Policy.

00:00:56.059 --> 00:01:00.829
Both have had experience in government, Bob
a few decades more.

00:01:00.829 --> 00:01:01.910
(Laughter.)

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:10.350
And what we do is spend a few minutes-full
advertising, this is the book.

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:12.380
We'll talk about it for 20 or so minutes.

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:17.700
Then we'll open it up and all of you will
have the not just opportunity but, I think,

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:23.740
moral obligation-(laughter)-to go out and
buy at least one copy, if not more.

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:30.950
I don't know about you; in our family Memorial
Day and July 4th are both major opportunities

00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:31.950
for book-giving.

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:32.950
(Laughter.)

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:37.229
And so you can take the pressure off yourselves
by-(laughter)-by doing this.

00:01:37.229 --> 00:01:41.320
It's too early, though, for Christmas.

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:51.009
OK, let's start with the obvious question-and
I don't know if you have a preferred sequencing-which

00:01:51.009 --> 00:01:55.790
is just to make sure we understand what we're
talking about, which is when we use the word-you

00:01:55.790 --> 00:01:57.700
know, we're all used to the phrase "geopolitics."

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:04.329
"Geoeconomics," shall we say, has a more recent
coinage or lineage.

00:02:04.329 --> 00:02:07.060
So when we use it, how do we understand it?

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:11.230
I'm not going to be referee here.

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:14.239
BLACKWILL: OK, I'll go ahead on this one.

00:02:14.239 --> 00:02:20.879
Well, let me just say thank you for hosting
us and thank you for all coming.

00:02:20.879 --> 00:02:25.569
It's a beautiful day out there and we all
wish we were out there.

00:02:25.569 --> 00:02:26.569
(Laughter.)

00:02:26.569 --> 00:02:29.710
So-
HAASS: We could move class to Central Park.

00:02:29.710 --> 00:02:36.459
BLACKWILL: I have this eerie capacity to look
into your inner beings here, especially since

00:02:36.459 --> 00:02:38.840
I was here last weekend in the rain.

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:48.300
But in any case, geoeconomics, this book emerged
from two years of study groups at the Council

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:50.450
on this subject.

00:02:50.450 --> 00:03:00.349
And as Jen and I were doing the initial research
we discovered, to our surprise, that the word

00:03:00.349 --> 00:03:09.730
"geoeconomics" has been used frequently, especially
since the late 1970s.

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:20.140
Ed Luttwak, the scholar-provocateur, may be
the first person to write about it more or

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:29.819
less in the way we think about it, but people
used it without defining it.

00:03:29.819 --> 00:03:38.129
So we set about, ourselves, first to the task
of, well, how are we going to define geoeconomics?

00:03:38.129 --> 00:03:43.270
And how are we going to distinguish it from
economics?

00:03:43.270 --> 00:03:47.420
And how are we going to distinguish it from
geopolitics?

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:58.239
And we arrived at this definition, which is
geoeconomics is the use of economic instruments

00:03:58.239 --> 00:04:01.980
for geopolitical purposes.

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:11.470
So not for economic purposes-not trade for
trade-but trade for geopolitical reasons.

00:04:11.470 --> 00:04:18.850
And the book is organized around that proposition,
that geoeconomics is the use of economic instruments

00:04:18.850 --> 00:04:21.989
for geopolitical purposes.

00:04:21.989 --> 00:04:32.150
And we discuss in the book who is good at
it now, doing this, who is not so good at

00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:38.060
it and why, and then-and I'll just give you
a hint.

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:44.080
Jen will speak to this in detail, but the
Chinese are very good at it and they are the

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:54.520
premier practitioners of this-of this discipline-the
Russians and the Gulf States and others.

00:04:54.520 --> 00:05:00.720
And we're not-we Americans are not now good
at it.

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:05.430
And we reach for the military instrument,
especially beginning in the period we developed

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:13.960
at length during the Vietnam War and thereafter,
and especially after 9/11.

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:22.300
And that's anomalous, because for most of
our history we were very good at it, using

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:26.910
economic instruments, all the way from the
Louisiana Purchase-where notice we didn't

00:05:26.910 --> 00:05:34.419
send an army to occupy it; we bought it-to
the Marshall Plan and so forth.

00:05:34.419 --> 00:05:39.000
And we used to be very good at it and we're
not any good anymore, or not very good.

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:40.970
And this is across administrations.

00:05:40.970 --> 00:05:48.449
So that's how the book is structured and we
invite you to have a look at it.

00:05:48.449 --> 00:05:54.490
HAASS: I'm going to ask in a minute to talk
about such questions about why the United

00:05:54.490 --> 00:05:59.010
States may not be as good at it as it was
or as it should be, but, Jen, let me ask you

00:05:59.010 --> 00:06:04.419
the question, some of-you know, Bob was there
at the time of the Louisiana Purchase-(laughter)-and

00:06:04.419 --> 00:06:09.259
worked on it, but those of us-
BLACKWILL: Yeah, I was working for you.

00:06:09.259 --> 00:06:10.889
(Laughter.)

00:06:10.889 --> 00:06:14.139
HAASS: OK.

00:06:14.139 --> 00:06:15.759
Touch�.

00:06:15.759 --> 00:06:17.389
Touch�.

00:06:17.389 --> 00:06:21.620
What are some more recent examples either
by the United States-just so we understand

00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:27.970
that-Bob gave us the definition, use of economic
tools for geopolitical purposes.

00:06:27.970 --> 00:06:31.530
What are some recent examples, be it by the
Chinese, the Russians, the United States,

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:35.310
anybody else, just so we make sure we kind
of have a good feel for what we're talking

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:36.310
about?

00:06:36.310 --> 00:06:37.310
HARRIS: Right.

00:06:37.310 --> 00:06:38.310
So first things first.

00:06:38.310 --> 00:06:44.139
I just wanted to add my thanks to Bob's to
both you and to Jim and to the Council for

00:06:44.139 --> 00:06:48.370
giving us the support and the space to write
this book.

00:06:48.370 --> 00:06:54.710
And I'd be remiss if I didn't mention my research
assistant, Allison Dorey, and Matt Lester

00:06:54.710 --> 00:06:59.780
as part of the village that it took to write
this book.

00:06:59.780 --> 00:07:07.360
But as Bob mentioned, if geoeconomics is more
or less geopolitics but by reference to a

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:12.800
suite of economic tools instead of the more
traditional diplomatic and military tools

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:17.120
that we think of when we think of states waging
geopolitics, what does this look like?

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:22.120
It really takes the form of everything from
trade and investment to monetary policy to

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:25.669
assistance, both economic and military.

00:07:25.669 --> 00:07:29.880
Military is probably the least interesting
of the lot, only there because money is fungible,

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:33.470
so the military assistance that we're giving
to Egypt is just freeing up budget space-

00:07:33.470 --> 00:07:35.449
HAASS: It's like economic support funds or
something.

00:07:35.449 --> 00:07:38.990
HARRIS: -right, just freeing up budget space
for them-to even aspects of the cyber domain.

00:07:38.990 --> 00:07:43.849
Certainly not all of the cyber domain do we
count as within the ambit of what we're concerned

00:07:43.849 --> 00:07:48.610
about-nefarious goings-on in the nuclear reactors
of Iran.

00:07:48.610 --> 00:07:53.590
That's going to be military and not something
we touch, but where we've seen countries like

00:07:53.590 --> 00:08:00.540
Russia take an opening shot in, say, the 2008
Russia-Georgia conflict by going after Georgia's

00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:02.630
banking sector first.

00:08:02.630 --> 00:08:08.169
They are venting a geopolitical disagreement
through economic conduits, and so that does

00:08:08.169 --> 00:08:10.810
sort of fit within our-
HAASS: Would economic sanctions then be a

00:08:10.810 --> 00:08:11.810
fairly common tool?

00:08:11.810 --> 00:08:12.810
HARRIS: Absolutely.

00:08:12.810 --> 00:08:14.330
That's one of the seven that we identify.

00:08:14.330 --> 00:08:15.330
HAASS: OK.

00:08:15.330 --> 00:08:19.160
I knew that because I read the book-(laughter)-but
I just wanted to-it's like "60 Minutes."

00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:21.090
BLACKWILL: Can I just chime in?

00:08:21.090 --> 00:08:24.460
It's not only one.

00:08:24.460 --> 00:08:33.539
These days it's really the only one that the
United States routinely reaches for, sometimes

00:08:33.539 --> 00:08:36.110
with good effect, sometimes with not.

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:44.090
We tend to sanction, as you know, many more
governments than any other country on earth.

00:08:44.090 --> 00:08:51.750
But it's the one that is used and the rest
of the instruments, the ones that Jen mentioned,

00:08:51.750 --> 00:08:53.660
have fallen into disuse.

00:08:53.660 --> 00:08:57.701
HAASS: Bob, you began with the idea that several
countries seem to do it better than we do

00:08:57.701 --> 00:08:59.850
it-we, the United States.

00:08:59.850 --> 00:09:04.360
What are some examples where other countries
have used geoeconomics successfully, and why-what

00:09:04.360 --> 00:09:06.560
do you think accounts for their success?

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:14.980
BLACKWILL: China-which I suppose a third of
our book is about China, which over the last

00:09:14.980 --> 00:09:21.820
couple of decades has used these economic
tools for geopolitical purposes most effectively.

00:09:21.820 --> 00:09:26.779
Let me say the book stresses-we stress in
the book they don't have a perfect record

00:09:26.779 --> 00:09:34.670
and they make mistakes the way governments
do, but let me give you some more specific

00:09:34.670 --> 00:09:36.480
examples to give some texture.

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:39.339
I'll give you three.

00:09:39.339 --> 00:09:47.490
If the Philippines acts with respect to the
South China Sea in ways that Beijing doesn't

00:09:47.490 --> 00:10:00.970
like, Philippine fruits and vegetables rot
on the ports-in the ports of China.

00:10:00.970 --> 00:10:12.420
If Japan does something in the East China
Sea that China doesn't like, Japan's exports-automobile

00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:17.820
exports to China substantially decline.

00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:28.450
China, to do the other side-the incentive
side, China has built something like 35 large

00:10:28.450 --> 00:10:43.060
soccer stadiums in Africa, curiously most
near the tribal homeland of the presidential

00:10:43.060 --> 00:10:46.260
leader of those countries.

00:10:46.260 --> 00:10:54.670
So there's an incentive part of this too,
and we develop this at length, but I'll try

00:10:54.670 --> 00:10:56.990
to vivify it.

00:10:56.990 --> 00:11:08.610
Lee Kwan Yew put it like this, that China
says to us in Singapore that all countries

00:11:08.610 --> 00:11:18.640
are created equal and need to be treated equally,
but then if we do something China doesn't

00:11:18.640 --> 00:11:26.560
like it says: You have just offended 1.15
billion Chinese.

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:31.260
Have you noticed the size of our market?

00:11:31.260 --> 00:11:33.579
Know your place.

00:11:33.579 --> 00:11:39.210
So this is quite systematic on the part of
the Chinese all around the world.

00:11:39.210 --> 00:11:46.540
The Norwegians discovered that when the leader
of Norway-prime minister of Norway saw the

00:11:46.540 --> 00:11:52.730
Dalai Lama, Norwegian trade with China tanked.

00:11:52.730 --> 00:11:54.630
And how can they do it?

00:11:54.630 --> 00:12:00.550
Well-and Jen can develop this, but they can
do it because they're an autocratic government.

00:12:00.550 --> 00:12:08.360
They have no countervailing pressures on them
and so they pull a trigger and do it.

00:12:08.360 --> 00:12:11.579
HAASS: Let me push back on that because-I'll
get ahead of myself.

00:12:11.579 --> 00:12:17.070
One of the things I was going to ask you about
is U.S. vulnerability to geoeconomic pressures.

00:12:17.070 --> 00:12:24.060
We'll take China sitting on-it's the largest
single foreign holder of American Treasurys-whatever,

00:12:24.060 --> 00:12:27.690
one-point-something trillion worth.

00:12:27.690 --> 00:12:32.010
So technically they could do certain things
like they could either stop buying them or

00:12:32.010 --> 00:12:35.250
start selling them or whatever in a crisis.

00:12:35.250 --> 00:12:40.420
On the other hand, their own holdings would
presumably lose value, plus they are vulnerable

00:12:40.420 --> 00:12:44.020
to a loss of access to the American market.

00:12:44.020 --> 00:12:48.830
So it seems to me that do we not have a form
of-I don't know if it's mutual assured destruction

00:12:48.830 --> 00:12:54.510
or, I guess, more positively, in the old days
we would have called it economic interdependence,

00:12:54.510 --> 00:13:00.970
that the Chinese in some ways are pretty needy
as well as-you know, they've got to worry,

00:13:00.970 --> 00:13:05.000
if you will, about their own vulnerability
as much as their ability to use this against

00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:06.000
others.

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:07.279
HARRIS: Sure.

00:13:07.279 --> 00:13:10.670
So your question is increasingly timely.

00:13:10.670 --> 00:13:15.880
As of last week or so, it became not academic
when the Saudis threatened to do exactly what

00:13:15.880 --> 00:13:21.920
you're describing, dump U.S. Treasury bills
if, indeed, Congress went through with some

00:13:21.920 --> 00:13:28.060
legislation that would pin liability for 9/11
on the Saudis, as Congress did.

00:13:28.060 --> 00:13:31.730
And so then the question becomes, how credible
of a threat is this?

00:13:31.730 --> 00:13:37.640
And normally I think the conventional wisdom
goes very much along the lines that you describe.

00:13:37.640 --> 00:13:40.579
This is economically irrational.

00:13:40.579 --> 00:13:45.920
Therefore, because they would take heavy economic
losses on the remaining value of whatever

00:13:45.920 --> 00:13:48.399
they don't dump, they wouldn't do it.

00:13:48.399 --> 00:13:52.070
That's true if you were looking at the world
through the lens of economic rationality,

00:13:52.070 --> 00:13:55.760
you know, with those interests at the fore.

00:13:55.760 --> 00:14:04.050
But if you are, you know, Xi Jinping and the
U.S. has escalated tensions around some maritime

00:14:04.050 --> 00:14:08.769
claims that you feel are first order to your
national security and you are looking for

00:14:08.769 --> 00:14:14.470
a way to underscore your displeasure with
Washington, and you have, say, I don't know,

00:14:14.470 --> 00:14:19.920
$2 billion that you want to budget for this,
where are you better off putting that $2 billion?

00:14:19.920 --> 00:14:25.070
In some fraction of your next aircraft carrier
installment when the U.S., even after that,

00:14:25.070 --> 00:14:27.800
will remain hyper-dominant in the military
realm?

00:14:27.800 --> 00:14:34.010
Or might you want to, I don't know, short
the U.S. housing recovery and your holdings

00:14:34.010 --> 00:14:35.971
of Fannie and Freddie?

00:14:35.971 --> 00:14:40.412
So I do think that once you take off the lens
of economic rationality and begin to look

00:14:40.412 --> 00:14:43.060
at it through a geopolitical lens, it's not
so clear.

00:14:43.060 --> 00:14:44.720
HAASS: Let's talk about-
BLACKWILL: Could I-

00:14:44.720 --> 00:14:45.720
HAASS: Sure.

00:14:45.720 --> 00:14:54.720
BLACKWILL: Could I just use what you asked,
the mutual assured-we remember that, despite

00:14:54.720 --> 00:15:02.639
the doctrine of mutual assured destruction,
we worried a lot about such crises getting

00:15:02.639 --> 00:15:10.690
out of control and mutual assured destruction
falling by the wayside in crisis decision

00:15:10.690 --> 00:15:11.690
making.

00:15:11.690 --> 00:15:12.690
HAASS: Right.

00:15:12.690 --> 00:15:18.470
BLACKWILL: And so even though we thought,
well, neither country was crazy enough to

00:15:18.470 --> 00:15:26.970
start a nuclear war because of mutual-we nevertheless
spent, whatever, thousands and thousands-tens

00:15:26.970 --> 00:15:32.010
of thousands of man and woman hours worrying
about it and figuring out what we would do

00:15:32.010 --> 00:15:39.411
if it broke down, and just what-imagine that,
in a crisis over the South China Sea, the

00:15:39.411 --> 00:15:44.990
Chinese don't sell Treasury bills; it's just
that there's a backgrounder from the central

00:15:44.990 --> 00:15:50.080
bank out of China that they were considering
it.

00:15:50.080 --> 00:15:53.100
What would happen to the stock market?

00:15:53.100 --> 00:16:00.690
And would China say, why don't we send these
Americans a little signal that there are wider

00:16:00.690 --> 00:16:07.019
implications of these actions they're taking
out in the South China Sea than simply the

00:16:07.019 --> 00:16:10.579
military demonstration of the fleet?

00:16:10.579 --> 00:16:12.339
HAASS: Yeah.

00:16:12.339 --> 00:16:17.589
Where mutual assured destruction failed was
in the decades right up to World War I.

00:16:17.589 --> 00:16:21.920
There was the Richard Cobden and John Bright
school, that economic interdependence-

00:16:21.920 --> 00:16:22.920
BLACKWILL: Exactly.

00:16:22.920 --> 00:16:25.149
HAASS: -would make war unthinkable because
everyone would pay a price.

00:16:25.149 --> 00:16:26.839
People seemed more than prepared to pay the
price-

00:16:26.839 --> 00:16:27.839
BLACKWILL: Yeah.

00:16:27.839 --> 00:16:28.839
HAASS: -when push came to shove.

00:16:28.839 --> 00:16:30.450
So I think you're on to something there.

00:16:30.450 --> 00:16:32.550
Let's talk about trade.

00:16:32.550 --> 00:16:37.270
I mean, right now you've got TPP sitting there.

00:16:37.270 --> 00:16:40.420
We don't know for how long.

00:16:40.420 --> 00:16:45.110
You defined geoeconomics at the outset as
economic tools for strategic geopolitical

00:16:45.110 --> 00:16:46.110
purposes.

00:16:46.110 --> 00:16:52.490
Wouldn't TPP qualify then, because whatever
its economic benefits, at least as important

00:16:52.490 --> 00:16:57.579
if not more important is strategic ties to
friends and allies.

00:16:57.579 --> 00:17:02.019
And conceivably, if China were involved in
phase two or three, it could be a form of

00:17:02.019 --> 00:17:03.019
economic interdependence.

00:17:03.019 --> 00:17:10.360
So is TPP a geoeconomic tool that we are allowing
to go unused?

00:17:10.360 --> 00:17:13.510
HARRIS: It could be.

00:17:13.510 --> 00:17:20.660
And I think the U.S.' treatment of this question
of TPP as a foreign policy tool is reflective

00:17:20.660 --> 00:17:24.329
of our broader discomfort with flexing economic
muscle today.

00:17:24.329 --> 00:17:30.960
And I think our basic sense-Bob, hopefully
we agree-is that you see a kind of schizophrenia

00:17:30.960 --> 00:17:32.990
with the U.S.' relationship with TPP.

00:17:32.990 --> 00:17:37.890
On one hand, we're quite comfortable marketing
TPP as a national security imperative.

00:17:37.890 --> 00:17:44.870
You saw Secretary of Defense Ash Carter come
to this very building and call TPP as important

00:17:44.870 --> 00:17:48.030
to the U.S. military as an aircraft carrier.

00:17:48.030 --> 00:17:53.100
President Obama has called it an explicit
referendum on U.S. leadership in Asia.

00:17:53.100 --> 00:17:56.530
The only problem is that's not the agreement
that we designed.

00:17:56.530 --> 00:18:04.419
In fact, I think we'd argue that if indeed
TPP were an answer to the strategic challenges

00:18:04.419 --> 00:18:07.740
that the U.S. is facing in Asia, you'd have
a very different agreement than the one that

00:18:07.740 --> 00:18:08.799
you have on your hands.

00:18:08.799 --> 00:18:14.580
And so it's really a matter of whether or
not U.S. officials are prioritizing U.S. geopolitical

00:18:14.580 --> 00:18:18.470
interests in the design choices that they're
making as they're negotiating these deals,

00:18:18.470 --> 00:18:23.740
separate and apart from when it comes time
to market them after the fact.

00:18:23.740 --> 00:18:27.630
And the effect of our marketing trade as a
national security imperative is not something

00:18:27.630 --> 00:18:28.850
unique to the Obama administration.

00:18:28.850 --> 00:18:30.490
This really goes back to NAFTA.

00:18:30.490 --> 00:18:35.510
You saw Colin Powell take the floor of NAFTA
and suggest he was going to have to raise

00:18:35.510 --> 00:18:38.330
divisions on our southern border if we didn't
pass it.

00:18:38.330 --> 00:18:42.830
BLACKWILL: Richard, could I just-
HAASS: Yeah, call out your-

00:18:42.830 --> 00:18:47.200
BLACKWILL: Just to be-a moment, just one specific
point.

00:18:47.200 --> 00:18:54.250
For example, I mentioned earlier-and the book
develops this in length-Chinese used coercively

00:18:54.250 --> 00:19:01.410
of its economic instruments virtually against
every country in Asia.

00:19:01.410 --> 00:19:13.580
Well, if the TPP had a geoeconomic strategic
purpose, surely you would say, among the countries

00:19:13.580 --> 00:19:21.490
negotiation, what are we going to put in the
TPP about this because we're all suffering?

00:19:21.490 --> 00:19:28.020
But the TPP was negotiated by trade experts,
and so there is, of course, not a word about

00:19:28.020 --> 00:19:29.020
it.

00:19:29.020 --> 00:19:37.929
But as Jen said, after the fact, when-with
an agreement negotiated entirely by trade

00:19:37.929 --> 00:19:46.970
experts on our side, entirely under trade
criteria, when they wished to sell it to Congress

00:19:46.970 --> 00:19:52.570
they discover a strategic purpose tacked on
to the end.

00:19:52.570 --> 00:19:56.860
HARRIS: And the problem is not so much that-I
mean, Bob and I didn't get our way on this

00:19:56.860 --> 00:20:02.059
question, that we did not build in defenses
to help steel U.S. allies against the sort

00:20:02.059 --> 00:20:04.559
of geoeconomic coercion they're facing in
Asia.

00:20:04.559 --> 00:20:05.990
The problem is that it wasn't even considered.

00:20:05.990 --> 00:20:09.980
I was there, you know, sort of negotiating
and sort of drafting some of the early chapters,

00:20:09.980 --> 00:20:13.860
and this didn't even cross the minds of people
in the State Department, let alone-

00:20:13.860 --> 00:20:15.220
HAASS: Can I say I don't understand?

00:20:15.220 --> 00:20:19.820
So what would be-because if TPP doesn't pass,
it will clearly have geopolitical consequences

00:20:19.820 --> 00:20:24.520
that are measurably adverse to the United
States.

00:20:24.520 --> 00:20:30.400
So what would be in a geoeconomic TPP that
is not in the TPP we have?

00:20:30.400 --> 00:20:31.400
How would it look different?

00:20:31.400 --> 00:20:33.800
HARRIS: So I think-three quick examples.

00:20:33.800 --> 00:20:40.480
One, you want truly sort of first-in-class
disciplines bringing state-owned enterprise

00:20:40.480 --> 00:20:41.870
to heel.

00:20:41.870 --> 00:20:47.420
And yes, it's true that we have a chapter
in TPP on state-owned enterprises.

00:20:47.420 --> 00:20:51.870
This is the first-ever named chapter on SOEs
a treaty agreement has ever had.

00:20:51.870 --> 00:20:58.480
But in point of fact, we have provisions in
the Singapore FTA on SOEs that are very bit

00:20:58.480 --> 00:21:02.520
as-actually far stronger than what we've managed
in TPP.

00:21:02.520 --> 00:21:08.450
And when we see the Chinese using their SOEs
as the main conduit through which they're

00:21:08.450 --> 00:21:12.151
changing facts in the water, the way in which
they're building islands in the South and

00:21:12.151 --> 00:21:17.880
East China Seas through their state-owned
enterprises-they use their state-owned oil

00:21:17.880 --> 00:21:19.750
company rigs as mobile territory.

00:21:19.750 --> 00:21:24.909
HAASS: Even though China is not a party to
TPP at this phase, do you still think we should

00:21:24.909 --> 00:21:29.080
have anticipated it and put in an SOE claim-
HARRIS: Is this about sort of building global

00:21:29.080 --> 00:21:31.557
norms for the 21st century, that would be-
BLACKWILL: Because of their behavior, because

00:21:31.557 --> 00:21:33.090
we know what their behavior has been.

00:21:33.090 --> 00:21:34.090
HAASS: I'm trying to understand.

00:21:34.090 --> 00:21:35.090
OK.

00:21:35.090 --> 00:21:36.090
BLACKWILL: We don't have to guess.

00:21:36.090 --> 00:21:37.090
HAASS: I'm trying to understand.

00:21:37.090 --> 00:21:38.090
BLACKWILL: There's lots of empirical evidence.

00:21:38.090 --> 00:21:41.820
HAASS: Look, implicit then in what you've
both said, it could or maybe should have done

00:21:41.820 --> 00:21:42.820
that.

00:21:42.820 --> 00:21:47.340
And, Bob, you basically, I think, made the
argument a minute or two ago that the USTR-not

00:21:47.340 --> 00:21:54.690
just Mike Froman but all of his predecessors-they
have a group of, largely, lawyers and trade

00:21:54.690 --> 00:21:58.600
experts, very talented.

00:21:58.600 --> 00:22:02.380
So is there something there for-I'm reading
into what you said but let me just guess that

00:22:02.380 --> 00:22:07.549
you think we need an approach to formulate,
in this case, trade policy but more broadly

00:22:07.549 --> 00:22:12.850
geoeconomic policy where non-economic considerations
or players are brought more centrally into

00:22:12.850 --> 00:22:13.850
the process.

00:22:13.850 --> 00:22:15.789
Is that a fair reading?

00:22:15.789 --> 00:22:17.250
BLACKWILL: Yes, absolutely.

00:22:17.250 --> 00:22:27.150
And we know that when there are discussions-interagency
discussions on this, those issues do not arise.

00:22:27.150 --> 00:22:28.390
And why is that?

00:22:28.390 --> 00:22:31.410
Well, there's a history to this.

00:22:31.410 --> 00:22:42.250
The Congress became anxious two-and-a-half
decades ago that trade and American jobs was

00:22:42.250 --> 00:22:49.960
not being sufficiently defended by the foreign
policy wonks at the State Department.

00:22:49.960 --> 00:23:01.320
So they created a separate entity, the trade
rep, and gave that entity such superintending

00:23:01.320 --> 00:23:11.419
powers that today the State Department's influence
on those negotiations is minimal-minimal,

00:23:11.419 --> 00:23:18.590
with the fear-if I can put it like this-those
weaklings over in the State Department are

00:23:18.590 --> 00:23:28.220
going to trade away American jobs for some
fuzzy-wuzzy geopolitical purposes.

00:23:28.220 --> 00:23:32.460
And so we've gone all the way down to the
spectrum.

00:23:32.460 --> 00:23:42.440
No fuzzy-wuzziness on our part, but we're
on a playing field or spectrum, or whatever

00:23:42.440 --> 00:23:48.340
metaphor you want to use, in which the Chinese
use a hundred percent of the field and we

00:23:48.340 --> 00:23:50.610
use about 35 (percent).

00:23:50.610 --> 00:23:59.730
And they understand this very well and exploit
it, and we're not in the game.

00:23:59.730 --> 00:24:06.470
HAASS: Let me ask a question I never thought
I was going to ask, but I'm going to ask it.

00:24:06.470 --> 00:24:07.470
And just hear me out.

00:24:07.470 --> 00:24:11.659
It will be my last question, you'll all be
relieved to know, then I'll open up, which

00:24:11.659 --> 00:24:20.169
is some of the candidates-in particular Mr.
Trump and Mr. Sanders-have highlighted trade,

00:24:20.169 --> 00:24:22.260
and particularly their opposition to it.

00:24:22.260 --> 00:24:28.090
Secretary of State Clinton has also come out
against TPP.

00:24:28.090 --> 00:24:33.799
In their own way-I'm clearly in favor of it,
but in their own way, are they practitioners

00:24:33.799 --> 00:24:41.679
of geoeconomics in their-in what it is they're-well,
I'm not sure they are, given your definition,

00:24:41.679 --> 00:24:46.059
but I just want to put that out there because
I expect they might say yes, but I'm curious

00:24:46.059 --> 00:24:47.720
whether you think, in fact, they are.

00:24:47.720 --> 00:24:49.890
And if not, why not?

00:24:49.890 --> 00:24:59.059
BLACKWILL: Well, let me chime in here, which
is-rock-ribbed Republican as I am, it's this:

00:24:59.059 --> 00:25:09.300
The arguments mostly that Mr. Trump and Senator
Sanders makes are trade for trade purposes.

00:25:09.300 --> 00:25:16.500
We're getting bad deals and we need to improve
the quality of our deals.

00:25:16.500 --> 00:25:23.880
With Mr. Trump in particular, it has an inverse
relationship to geopolitics, because what

00:25:23.880 --> 00:25:32.160
he proposes we can lose our alliances; that
is to say, confronting the Japanese and saying,

00:25:32.160 --> 00:25:36.429
if you don't do what we want we'll withdraw
from the alliance.

00:25:36.429 --> 00:25:46.880
So our proposition about geoeconomics is-and
we have 20 prescriptions in the penultimate

00:25:46.880 --> 00:25:52.610
chapter of the book, which says, here is what
the next administration ought to do with respect

00:25:52.610 --> 00:26:05.190
to geoeconomics, but none of it is to threaten
our closest allies with termination of our

00:26:05.190 --> 00:26:12.789
alliance systems on which we have depended
since the end of the Second World War for

00:26:12.789 --> 00:26:16.539
promoting stability around the world.

00:26:16.539 --> 00:26:24.320
So Mr. Trump and Mr. Sanders both say we need
to have better deals.

00:26:24.320 --> 00:26:33.770
Well, we don't-Jen and I don't reject better
deals, but better deals at the expense-a methodology

00:26:33.770 --> 00:26:40.059
of which ruptures our relationships with our
closest friends and allies is exactly the

00:26:40.059 --> 00:26:44.309
opposite of what we think geoeconomics ought
to produce.

00:26:44.309 --> 00:26:45.760
HAASS: OK.

00:26:45.760 --> 00:26:51.159
I could ask a lot more questions, but I'll
show uncharacteristic restraint.

00:26:51.159 --> 00:26:52.159
(Laughter.)

00:26:52.159 --> 00:26:56.549
We have microphones, I expect, in the room,
so just raise your hand and I'll call on you.

00:26:56.549 --> 00:27:00.260
If you keep it short, they keep it short,
we'll get quite a few in.

00:27:00.260 --> 00:27:01.260
Sir.

00:27:01.260 --> 00:27:02.880
Just let us know who you are.

00:27:02.880 --> 00:27:03.880
Q: Thank you.

00:27:03.880 --> 00:27:04.880
Scott Helfstein, BNY Mellon.

00:27:04.880 --> 00:27:08.500
Appreciate the work you guys have done and
the time you've spent tonight.

00:27:08.500 --> 00:27:14.320
Taking into account what you said about China's
success in this space, from a conceptual standpoint

00:27:14.320 --> 00:27:22.130
is it possible for an advocate-a country that
is an advocate of free trade to put together

00:27:22.130 --> 00:27:28.910
a strategic framework that is consistent with
geoeconomics that you're saying could be practiced?

00:27:28.910 --> 00:27:29.910
HAASS: Good question.

00:27:29.910 --> 00:27:30.910
Go ahead, please.

00:27:30.910 --> 00:27:31.910
HARRIS: Sure.

00:27:31.910 --> 00:27:38.520
So I think a lot of the discomfort with a
more assertive brand of American geoeconomics

00:27:38.520 --> 00:27:43.919
comes from a tendency to put laissez-faire
liberal economics on one end of a spectrum

00:27:43.919 --> 00:27:47.650
and sort of mercantilism on the other end
of the spectrum.

00:27:47.650 --> 00:27:50.320
Richard mentioned Richard Cobden earlier.

00:27:50.320 --> 00:27:57.440
Actually, to me this is a false equivalence
and it turns on a misreading of the early

00:27:57.440 --> 00:27:58.700
economic liberals.

00:27:58.700 --> 00:28:02.799
Richard Cobden, you know, said: No free trade
in cutting throats.

00:28:02.799 --> 00:28:12.649
Adam Smith also looked at sort of laissez-faire
economics as a theory about how to best serve

00:28:12.649 --> 00:28:15.179
national interests and national power writ
large.

00:28:15.179 --> 00:28:20.909
And so their disagreements with mercantilists
really turned on how, not whether, to practice

00:28:20.909 --> 00:28:21.909
geoeconomics.

00:28:21.909 --> 00:28:26.480
So I think kind of rereading our economic
history as Americans today is probably step

00:28:26.480 --> 00:28:27.570
one.

00:28:27.570 --> 00:28:33.200
But absolutely I think, you know, there's
a lot of room on the table for a more assertive

00:28:33.200 --> 00:28:39.180
brand of American geoeconomics that is consistent
with our values and our-the fact that we were

00:28:39.180 --> 00:28:44.679
founded as a nation begun as an experiment
in limited government, and we are going to

00:28:44.679 --> 00:28:49.710
be constrained in some of the activities in
this geoeconomic space that the Chinese and

00:28:49.710 --> 00:28:51.840
the Russians are just simply less constrained
in.

00:28:51.840 --> 00:28:56.419
But I think, in fact, a lot of the things
that we could put into TPP that would make

00:28:56.419 --> 00:29:02.120
it a more geoeconomic sort of savvy trade
deal are things that also row in the direction

00:29:02.120 --> 00:29:05.299
of enhanced U.S. economic stability.

00:29:05.299 --> 00:29:07.710
So I mentioned SOEs earlier.

00:29:07.710 --> 00:29:09.130
Currency also comes to mind.

00:29:09.130 --> 00:29:16.310
There is a fairly robust bipartisan consensus
in the Congress to include currency within

00:29:16.310 --> 00:29:18.880
the realm of a trade concern.

00:29:18.880 --> 00:29:26.090
And it's also the case that China is financing
a lot of its going-out strategy right now-its

00:29:26.090 --> 00:29:30.690
new One Belt One Road initiative that some
of you might have heard of-through its state

00:29:30.690 --> 00:29:34.409
policy banks, which happen to be financed
through its current account surplus, which

00:29:34.409 --> 00:29:37.980
is-you know, it's U.S. Treasury bills by another
name.

00:29:37.980 --> 00:29:44.200
And so whether you are, you know, concerned
about this question of currency on a sheer

00:29:44.200 --> 00:29:49.860
economic vein or view it as economic security,
or more on the geopolitical rationale, I think

00:29:49.860 --> 00:29:56.429
both sort of lenses will get you to, you know,
a really sensible position in putting currency

00:29:56.429 --> 00:29:57.429
as a trade issue.

00:29:57.429 --> 00:30:01.450
HAASS: But coming back to the question, there
is a tension between, if you will, unadulterated

00:30:01.450 --> 00:30:04.049
pure free trade and geoeconomics.

00:30:04.049 --> 00:30:08.080
You're basically saying whatever possible
strategic upsides we may get through free

00:30:08.080 --> 00:30:12.970
trade through interdependence in many cases
will be overwhelmed by other considerations,

00:30:12.970 --> 00:30:18.710
so we ought to choose to use trade as a principle-as
an instrument of statecraft, even if it means

00:30:18.710 --> 00:30:19.750
we're not pure free traders.

00:30:19.750 --> 00:30:20.750
That's fine.

00:30:20.750 --> 00:30:25.580
BLACKWILL: Yeah, but not-yes, but not the
dominant unitary instrument.

00:30:25.580 --> 00:30:35.169
It's just we plea in the book, factor the
geopolitical into your policies over trade;

00:30:35.169 --> 00:30:43.730
not that it dominates necessarily but just
have, in the process, the assurance that you're

00:30:43.730 --> 00:30:49.679
addressing these issues-addressing them, and
then let's see how the interagency process

00:30:49.679 --> 00:30:51.830
balanced out.

00:30:51.830 --> 00:31:01.350
In the last-pick a number-30 years, that consideration,
which would have been odd to George Marshall,

00:31:01.350 --> 00:31:02.350
has disappeared.

00:31:02.350 --> 00:31:08.779
HAASS: Just so I understand-sorry to interrupt-so
when we, for example, gave the original instructions

00:31:08.779 --> 00:31:13.820
for TPP, which was, what, about 15 years ago-
BLACKWILL: Yeah.

00:31:13.820 --> 00:31:18.480
HAASS: -I don't know; I lost my sense of time-there
was not an NSC kind of interagency meeting

00:31:18.480 --> 00:31:24.220
saying that we-that it was basically done
simply in the-through an economic kind of-

00:31:24.220 --> 00:31:25.220
BLACKWILL: Yeah.

00:31:25.220 --> 00:31:28.820
HARRIS: Larry Summers, I think it was, called
it a trade agreement with Brunei-(laughter)-in

00:31:28.820 --> 00:31:29.830
the first instance.

00:31:29.830 --> 00:31:35.010
You know, it has grown into, you know, a different
animal altogether from its early origins.

00:31:35.010 --> 00:31:36.750
HAASS: Oh, interesting.

00:31:36.750 --> 00:31:37.750
Sure.

00:31:37.750 --> 00:31:40.250
You guys fight it out.

00:31:40.250 --> 00:31:41.250
(Laughter.)

00:31:41.250 --> 00:31:43.159
Q: Ruben Jeffery, Rockefeller.

00:31:43.159 --> 00:31:44.410
Bob, Jennifer, thank you.

00:31:44.410 --> 00:31:45.410
This is a great subject.

00:31:45.410 --> 00:31:46.410
Thanks for taking it on.

00:31:46.410 --> 00:31:50.539
You know, the United States, we're neither
a centrally planned economy nor a dictatorship,

00:31:50.539 --> 00:31:51.539
at least not yet.

00:31:51.539 --> 00:31:58.799
And in fact, when it comes to these economic
matters, we couldn't be more diffuse in terms

00:31:58.799 --> 00:31:59.840
of our organizational structure.

00:31:59.840 --> 00:32:04.429
You know, you've got the USTR, you've got
Treasury, you know, the financial market regulatory

00:32:04.429 --> 00:32:08.460
agencies, you've got the Department of Energy,
all this stuff, and there's economic opponents

00:32:08.460 --> 00:32:11.210
of every-just about every branch of the federal
government.

00:32:11.210 --> 00:32:16.440
Are there any sort of organizational prescriptions
you make in the book as to how to better kind

00:32:16.440 --> 00:32:20.900
of structure ourselves for economic diplomacy,
if you will, and statecraft?

00:32:20.900 --> 00:32:23.990
You know, we've got a Defense Department,
we've got, you know, a Foreign Service, and

00:32:23.990 --> 00:32:27.940
they're all very structured, but on the economic
side it's so diffuse.

00:32:27.940 --> 00:32:31.830
How do you sort of bring it in so you get
a more holistic approach?

00:32:31.830 --> 00:32:35.929
HARRIS: Never an easy question.

00:32:35.929 --> 00:32:40.169
I could go on longer than you probably would
want to hear, so let me just give you a couple

00:32:40.169 --> 00:32:45.659
of kinds of things that we could begin to
move on.

00:32:45.659 --> 00:32:50.470
Absolutely, you know, you know the inside
of the Situation Room better than I do.

00:32:50.470 --> 00:32:54.940
And it's the case that I think what it means
to be qualified for a lot of the jobs that

00:32:54.940 --> 00:33:02.770
sit at the intersection of economics and foreign
policy tend to involve pedigrees from, you

00:33:02.770 --> 00:33:07.160
know, investment banks and a command of the
financial sector.

00:33:07.160 --> 00:33:15.730
And often with that comes a set of-not always,
but a set of priors about whether markets

00:33:15.730 --> 00:33:19.850
should be subjugated into a tool of foreign
policy or not.

00:33:19.850 --> 00:33:26.659
So I think, you know, where you stand on any
given issue in U.S. foreign policy apparatus

00:33:26.659 --> 00:33:34.230
is a product of where you sit and we need
to create a mandate for the use of economic

00:33:34.230 --> 00:33:35.230
tools in this way.

00:33:35.230 --> 00:33:37.280
HAASS: Well, sure, but aren't certain-but
weren't certain people dual-hatted to be both

00:33:37.280 --> 00:33:42.070
in the National Economic Council and on the
National Security Council exactly for this

00:33:42.070 --> 00:33:43.070
reason?

00:33:43.070 --> 00:33:50.330
HARRIS: They are, but I think often they are
coming from a background that lends itself

00:33:50.330 --> 00:33:55.929
to an expertise in finance, and that tends
to-they gravitate towards, you know, planning

00:33:55.929 --> 00:33:56.929
the G-20.

00:33:56.929 --> 00:34:00.309
And there's more than enough work to be done
and these people are stretched thin, but I

00:34:00.309 --> 00:34:04.690
think what gets left out are, you know, the
more strategic use of-the sort of transactional

00:34:04.690 --> 00:34:05.690
use of those tools.

00:34:05.690 --> 00:34:09.690
HAASS: Just out of curiosity, has anyone from
State or Defense ever been seconded, almost

00:34:09.690 --> 00:34:13.110
like pol ads are seconded to every joint command?

00:34:13.110 --> 00:34:19.879
Do you ever have the equivalent of a foreign
policy ad or strategic ad who would be seconded

00:34:19.879 --> 00:34:20.879
to USTR?

00:34:20.879 --> 00:34:22.790
Has that ever come up?

00:34:22.790 --> 00:34:23.790
HARRIS: Transaction costs.

00:34:23.790 --> 00:34:28.940
It has happened but it usually involves so
much negotiation that it's more of a one-off

00:34:28.940 --> 00:34:32.010
and, you know, it's not like a routine, systematic
thing.

00:34:32.010 --> 00:34:35.000
But just two more quick points on this, because
this is important.

00:34:35.000 --> 00:34:37.149
I think Congress also has to be brought into
this.

00:34:37.149 --> 00:34:42.710
And when you look at a lot of the failings
or our Arab Spring policy, it's not even so

00:34:42.710 --> 00:34:46.179
much a dollars-and-cents issue, although that
is there.

00:34:46.179 --> 00:34:52.929
When I sort of-when the president first came
out with his sort of theory of the case in

00:34:52.929 --> 00:34:56.770
May 2011 on the Arab Spring, it sounded a
lot like what we were calling for.

00:34:56.770 --> 00:34:58.760
It was a billion-dollar debt swap for Egypt.

00:34:58.760 --> 00:35:02.510
It was enterprise funds for Egypt and Tunisia,
modeled after ones that had worked pretty

00:35:02.510 --> 00:35:07.900
well in early post-Soviet days in the '90s.

00:35:07.900 --> 00:35:09.300
It was loan guarantees.

00:35:09.300 --> 00:35:13.910
The only problem was that when I left the
State Department three years later, those

00:35:13.910 --> 00:35:15.740
enterprise funds had yet to cut their first
checks.

00:35:15.740 --> 00:35:20.670
We as an administration were still arguing
with ourselves about how to spend this billion

00:35:20.670 --> 00:35:25.250
dollars, long after the Egyptian counterparts
on the other side of the table had begun to

00:35:25.250 --> 00:35:28.810
rotate, meaning new facts on the ground had
begun to outpace the U.S. government's ability

00:35:28.810 --> 00:35:29.810
to respond.

00:35:29.810 --> 00:35:34.140
And a lot of that came down to congressional
authorization to spend the money that we already

00:35:34.140 --> 00:35:35.770
had and do those appropriations.

00:35:35.770 --> 00:35:42.400
BLACKWILL: Could I say one other thing about
the Congress, which is just an empirical point?

00:35:42.400 --> 00:35:51.880
If you were to catalogue, in the last 20 years,
the number of congressional hearings on security

00:35:51.880 --> 00:36:04.890
issues and the number of congressional hearings
on economic instruments as a tool of American

00:36:04.890 --> 00:36:09.450
strategic purpose, there are basically none
in the latter.

00:36:09.450 --> 00:36:20.090
Our Congress is preoccupied-and I'm not saying
that it shouldn't be, but it's almost exclusively

00:36:20.090 --> 00:36:25.680
preoccupied with how many special operators
are there in Syria?

00:36:25.680 --> 00:36:28.950
And, oh, well, there are 250.

00:36:28.950 --> 00:36:34.160
Well, we want to have a hearing to make clear
it should be 375.

00:36:34.160 --> 00:36:42.160
Meanwhile, next door, Jordan, a key to the
future of the Middle East, an American ally,

00:36:42.160 --> 00:36:50.180
is breaking apart economically because of
Syrian refugees and the enormous pressures

00:36:50.180 --> 00:36:51.839
on Jordan.

00:36:51.839 --> 00:36:59.380
And what are we helping economically to try
to buttress the Jordanian regime?

00:36:59.380 --> 00:37:01.790
It's pitiful.

00:37:01.790 --> 00:37:10.350
And so you're not going to find hearings over
this long period along the lines that we recommend.

00:37:10.350 --> 00:37:13.430
HAASS: So I'm just going to offer 2 cents
there.

00:37:13.430 --> 00:37:20.930
In 1990 or '91, the United States forgave
$7 billion in Egyptian debt at the beginning

00:37:20.930 --> 00:37:23.510
of the-and there were hearings.

00:37:23.510 --> 00:37:27.040
David Welch actually was the person who handled
it at the NSC.

00:37:27.040 --> 00:37:30.170
And then more recently Bob Corker was credited
to hold hearings slightly different on the

00:37:30.170 --> 00:37:35.829
strategic implications of the U.S.-of U.S.
indebtedness and taking a long-term look about

00:37:35.829 --> 00:37:40.270
what-almost because we-and one of the issues
they came up was whether we were making ourselves

00:37:40.270 --> 00:37:45.010
strategically vulnerable because of our dependence
on the influence of money.

00:37:45.010 --> 00:37:47.359
Aman (ph)?

00:37:47.359 --> 00:37:48.541
Q: Thank you.

00:37:48.541 --> 00:37:51.260
Thank you very much for addressing this topic.

00:37:51.260 --> 00:37:56.329
Talking about vulnerability and playing better
defense-and I want to couch it in the European

00:37:56.329 --> 00:38:01.280
Union because is that structure something
that makes them more vulnerable?

00:38:01.280 --> 00:38:06.880
They have diffuse political goals and decision
making, but economically it's a big group

00:38:06.880 --> 00:38:10.700
that, you know, from far it looked like the
Russians really take advantage of that, for

00:38:10.700 --> 00:38:11.700
example.

00:38:11.700 --> 00:38:18.160
Can you comment on that and maybe what could
be done to make that a better player defensively?

00:38:18.160 --> 00:38:28.030
BLACKWILL: It's a telling point because the
Ukrainian crisis vivified what has long been

00:38:28.030 --> 00:38:39.910
a suspicion of mine, that outside EU headquarters
in Brussels there is an electronic means of

00:38:39.910 --> 00:38:45.890
establishing whether the people who try to
enter the building have a strategic bone in

00:38:45.890 --> 00:38:47.430
their body.

00:38:47.430 --> 00:38:48.430
(Laughter.)

00:38:48.430 --> 00:38:55.570
And if they do, they get a mild shock if they
try to enter, because look at the package

00:38:55.570 --> 00:39:05.880
that they offered the Ukrainians, which caused-started
the series of events that caused the revolution

00:39:05.880 --> 00:39:09.630
and everything that has ensued thereafter.

00:39:09.630 --> 00:39:19.320
So the commission seems incapable of thinking
of geoeconomics the way we have argued when

00:39:19.320 --> 00:39:23.470
it deals with foreign countries.

00:39:23.470 --> 00:39:36.410
And I don't know-since Europe is now maybe
the primary sick man anywhere-there's chaos

00:39:36.410 --> 00:39:44.510
many places but Europe is sick-I'm not-I'm
not optimistic they're going to fix this problem.

00:39:44.510 --> 00:39:45.510
Jen?

00:39:45.510 --> 00:39:54.230
HARRIS: I think a lot of it is chalked up
to first principles and constitutional constraints

00:39:54.230 --> 00:39:59.869
that you see some of to a lesser extent in
the American context, that you're not going

00:39:59.869 --> 00:40:02.730
to fix with the Europeans, but they do a lot
of it to themselves.

00:40:02.730 --> 00:40:08.970
There's no constitutional reason in Europe
why they have set-they have come to a default

00:40:08.970 --> 00:40:15.180
setting around sanctions that requires them
to affirmatively re-up the European side of

00:40:15.180 --> 00:40:21.370
the U.S.-EU sanctions on Russia right now,
just as one example where that would be the

00:40:21.370 --> 00:40:28.460
kind of thing that I would hope we would prioritize
in the next administration, beginning to develop

00:40:28.460 --> 00:40:35.400
an economic relationship with the EU that
there's some resemblance to the military relationship

00:40:35.400 --> 00:40:36.400
that we have in NATO.

00:40:36.400 --> 00:40:38.240
HAASS: There may be time for two more.

00:40:38.240 --> 00:40:40.552
BLACKWILL: Richard, could I just say-
HAASS: One more.

00:40:40.552 --> 00:40:41.552
(Laughter.)

00:40:41.552 --> 00:40:49.540
HARRIS: -one, that I liked the question-okay,
but I want to-now, we stress the need-if the

00:40:49.540 --> 00:40:57.090
next American president were to be sympathetic
to the ideas, we really have to proselytize

00:40:57.090 --> 00:41:05.650
them, not just in the Congress and home but
we have to go to our alliances and our friends,

00:41:05.650 --> 00:41:14.290
who mostly don't think in this way now, and
try to persuade them that this is a powerful

00:41:14.290 --> 00:41:19.180
potential instrument of alliance solidarity
and purposes.

00:41:19.180 --> 00:41:21.619
After all, we have the biggest economy in
the world.

00:41:21.619 --> 00:41:23.950
We're the richest country in the world.

00:41:23.950 --> 00:41:28.910
We have Japan, and we go through the size
of these economies.

00:41:28.910 --> 00:41:33.410
But they don't think like that and we would
have to, led by the president-and it would

00:41:33.410 --> 00:41:40.680
have to be led by the president-say, let's
not just talk about the security dimensions

00:41:40.680 --> 00:41:46.890
of the U.S.-Japan alliance or NATO or whatever
you want to talk about on the military side;

00:41:46.890 --> 00:41:52.640
let's talk about how geoeconomic instruments
can strengthen these relationships.

00:41:52.640 --> 00:41:55.680
HAASS: I'm going to take very quick questions,
John and Jen (sp).

00:41:55.680 --> 00:41:58.020
I'm going to collect these two questions-quick
questions, quick answers.

00:41:58.020 --> 00:41:59.940
And I apologize.

00:41:59.940 --> 00:42:01.089
Q: Thank you.

00:42:01.089 --> 00:42:03.880
And thank you, Mr. Ambassador and Ms. Harris,
for writing the book.

00:42:03.880 --> 00:42:07.910
John Sakowicz, KMEC Radio in Mendocino County.

00:42:07.910 --> 00:42:14.840
In speaking with Senator Feinstein and Representative
Mike Thompson, both of whom sit on Intelligence

00:42:14.840 --> 00:42:21.069
Committees, they have been thinking long and
hard about the prospect of China dumping Treasurys

00:42:21.069 --> 00:42:26.560
and also Saudi Arabia dumping Treasurys, but
their view is that it's not really-it's really

00:42:26.560 --> 00:42:33.800
more of an asymmetrical comparison to a nuclear
mutually assured destruction because, in the

00:42:33.800 --> 00:42:39.500
swaps-in the area of swaps and derivatives
you can short-you can make money shorting

00:42:39.500 --> 00:42:40.500
markets.

00:42:40.500 --> 00:42:47.470
So China or Saudi Arabia can hire Goldman
Sachs and be on both sides of the trade.

00:42:47.470 --> 00:42:52.070
And in fact, Goldman Sachs was on both sides
of the Greek default trade.

00:42:52.070 --> 00:42:58.849
So my question is, do you agree that there's
a certain asymmetrical nature to this new

00:42:58.849 --> 00:43:01.700
era of geoeconomic warfare?

00:43:01.700 --> 00:43:02.700
HAASS: Hold your answer.

00:43:02.700 --> 00:43:04.210
Let's get Jen's (sp) last question.

00:43:04.210 --> 00:43:06.900
And we'll give you a chance-you can choose
whichever question is easier.

00:43:06.900 --> 00:43:08.430
Q: Thanks.

00:43:08.430 --> 00:43:14.130
So my question is, how do we protect our country
from the devastating geoeconomics effects

00:43:14.130 --> 00:43:20.349
on our economy when the unprecedented money
printing and debt since 2008 inevitably comes

00:43:20.349 --> 00:43:21.710
home to roost?

00:43:21.710 --> 00:43:23.650
HAASS: Related questions.

00:43:23.650 --> 00:43:24.650
Rare.

00:43:24.650 --> 00:43:25.650
BLACKWILL: Jen?

00:43:25.650 --> 00:43:26.650
HAASS: Okay.

00:43:26.650 --> 00:43:28.220
Take it away, Jen, Bob.

00:43:28.220 --> 00:43:35.319
HARRIS: I think both of your questions touch
on something important, which is the-a lot

00:43:35.319 --> 00:43:36.400
of what's new here.

00:43:36.400 --> 00:43:40.710
You know, certain people, other books, have
written about aspects, singular dimensions

00:43:40.710 --> 00:43:47.089
of what we're talking about, but the pace
and the depth and the liquidity of global

00:43:47.089 --> 00:43:51.250
capital markets is acting as a force multiplier
across all of these instruments.

00:43:51.250 --> 00:43:56.300
And so my short answer to your question on
vulnerability and whether it's asymmetric

00:43:56.300 --> 00:43:57.300
is, absolutely.

00:43:57.300 --> 00:44:02.460
And I haven't seen nearly enough thinking
go into, you know, what sort of prophylactic

00:44:02.460 --> 00:44:04.530
protections we could be putting in.

00:44:04.530 --> 00:44:11.920
And again, I think it derives from this discomfort
of even thinking in these terms, point one.

00:44:11.920 --> 00:44:16.910
And then, you know, certainly our monetary
policy has had to do some unprecedented heroics.

00:44:16.910 --> 00:44:18.870
And I would call them heroics.

00:44:18.870 --> 00:44:26.089
It probably depends on which side of the political
aisle you fall on this.

00:44:26.089 --> 00:44:32.960
But how this redounds to U.S. economic health
I think is the broader question and, you know,

00:44:32.960 --> 00:44:34.210
by several measures.

00:44:34.210 --> 00:44:39.450
You've seen, you know, U.S. deficits actually
as a percentage of GDP-which to me is the

00:44:39.450 --> 00:44:41.630
more salient reference point-actually come
down.

00:44:41.630 --> 00:44:49.030
So I think we are-both by virtue of what we
averted in the monetary policy positions that

00:44:49.030 --> 00:44:54.380
we took and by some more conventional measures
like deficits as a percentage of GDP-in stronger

00:44:54.380 --> 00:44:57.170
terms certainly then we were eight years ago.

00:44:57.170 --> 00:44:58.170
HAASS: Bob?

00:44:58.170 --> 00:44:59.170
OK.

00:44:59.170 --> 00:45:07.519
So, look, let me again congratulate Ambassador
Blackwill and Professor Harris.

00:45:07.519 --> 00:45:08.680
This is an important book.

00:45:08.680 --> 00:45:10.260
It actually-I think you got the taste for
it.

00:45:10.260 --> 00:45:14.441
It takes a big idea and it really develops
it.

00:45:14.441 --> 00:45:16.980
And it does it in a way that's accessible.

00:45:16.980 --> 00:45:20.610
If you saw the piece in Foreign Affairs, you'll
know what I mean.

00:45:20.610 --> 00:45:27.640
But it's an interesting combination of conceptual
work with practical and applied histories.

00:45:27.640 --> 00:45:30.740
So again, I commend the authors.

00:45:30.740 --> 00:45:31.740
I thank them for it.

00:45:31.740 --> 00:45:35.819
It's a real important contribution to the
literature, and I would recommend that those

00:45:35.819 --> 00:45:42.010
of you who have not bought it do so.

00:45:42.010 --> 00:45:43.760
And if you do do that, then read it.

00:45:43.760 --> 00:45:45.130
You'll gain a lot from it.

00:45:45.130 --> 00:45:46.150
So again, thanks to you both.

00:45:46.150 --> 00:45:47.150
(Applause.)

00:45:47.150 --> 00:45:47.151
(END)

